\f0\fs22 \'93When I set the model\'92s gravitational constant slightly higher than ours,\'94 Ortolan recounted, \'93it resolved the blurred markings in this sample.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 A physicist leaned forward. \'93How many samples?\'94\
\'93Two. The other required a lower value.\'94 Ortolan displayed the second sample.\
\'93Assuming your model is right.\'94\
\'93Yes.\'94\
Johnny looked between the two images. \'93Different universes.\'94\
Ortolan nodded. \'93Consistent with that interpretation.\'94 \
\'93Different universes,\'94 Johnny repeated. He stood, but seemed uncertain what to do once upright. \'93That\'92s\'85 good work, Doctor.\'94\
Ortolan waited.\
\'93Very good work.\'94\
Sarah watched Johnny lean across the table and offer his hand. Ortolan took it.\
\'93You\'92ve made my day.\'94\
\'93Thank you, Johnny.\'94\
The handshake stopped.\
\'93What about the other constants?\'94 Johnny asked. \'93Have you checked those?\'94\
\'93Not yet.\'94\
\'93So we shouldn\'92t get ahead of ourselves.\'94\
Ortolan withdrew his hand.\
\'93Can we process the remaining samples?\'94\
\'93Now that the correction works, we can divide the remaining samples among our team.\'94\
Johnny looked around the table. The mathematician and Vilnius were working on the same tablet. The conservator had returned to the enlarged grooves. The Egyptologist was writing something the cryptologist apparently disagreed with.\
\'93Good,\'94 Johnny said. \'93We\'92ll keep going.\'94\
Sarah waited until Ortolan had begun answering the questions of half the physicists, then beckoned Johnny closer.\
\'93Has Chilo seen these?\'94\
\'93Why would I show him?\'94\
\'93He supplied your disc.\'94\
Johnny had been betrayed. \'93He didn\'92t supply an instruction manual.\'94\
\'93No,\'94 Sarah said. \'93He didn\'92t.\'94\
Johnny had half turned away.\
\'93I want the Sages here,\'94 Sarah told him.
